+//------------------------------------------------------------------
+// The fill collector
+//------------------------------------------------------------------
+// A cache line fill asks main_bus for the eight words of one 32-byte line,
+// one word at a time. Each word is two halfword beats, so the fill costs 16
+// arbiter grants and 16 single SDRAM accesses; one 16-beat burst of the same
+// line costs one grant. So the second read that lands in the line the
+// previous read landed in turns into a burst for that whole line, and the
+// fill's remaining reads are answered out of what it brought back. A lone
+// read costs exactly what it did before -- nothing is fetched ahead of a
+// read that has no follower.
+//
+// This is 16 bytes of memory data -- one half of a line -- not a cache, and
+// four words is enough for both halves of the same fill: the first read of
+// each half becomes an 8-beat burst, every read after it is a hit. A whole
+// line would cost three grants as well, so the two halves are bought with
+// 128 flops instead of 256 and the same transaction count.
+//
+// It is only ever read back by an access to the same 16 bytes, and any
+// completed write into main RAM from any master drops it. That is sound
+// because main_bus is the only path a main-RAM write can take. The burst is
+// atomic, which is stronger than the four separate reads it replaces: a
